    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Brice Turang** stands out as our clear anchor with a 63.99 efficiency score. While his status is technically \"Lineup Pending,\" the assumption of the #2 spot against Sasaki provides a massive boost. **Brice Turang** is a priority start not just for his expected volume, but because the matchup profiles perfectly for his aggressive base-running; the combination of a high-floor base projection and the \"Slow SP Delivery\" bonus makes him our best bet to steal a bag and provide value in a category where we remain hyper-focused. Conversely, while **Josh Naylor** (55.47) and **Travis Bazzana** (53.06) grade as solid, they face stiffer competition. **Josh Naylor** is fighting through a disadvantageous platoon matchup against Fried, which caps his ceiling, while **Travis Bazzana** benefits significantly from a favorable park factor and a mid-rotation matchup against Montero that allows him to outperform his base projection.\n\nThe most difficult decision today involved the bottom of our active roster, where **Seiya Suzuki** (51.86) barely edged out the rest of the utility considerations. **Seiya Suzuki** is buoyed heavily by his confirmed #2 spot in the order, which mitigates the negative impact of facing a tough arm in Cavalli. We are managing a depleted bench due to the ongoing absences of **James Wood** and **Vinnie Pasquantino**, both of whom remain on the IL, while **Corbin Carroll** and **Tyler Soderstrom** are unavailable due to team off-days. With **Jose Caballero** (42.15) rounding out the active lineup despite a difficult matchup against Gilbert, our depth is thin. We are monitoring the weather closely, but the primary risk remains the high-level arms on the mound; if **Jose Caballero** struggles to navigate the strike zone against Gilbert, our reliance on the speed profiles of **Brice Turang** and our remaining available bench assets like **Mickey Moniak**, **Austin Riley**, and **Giancarlo Stanton** will be crucial to keeping our 5x5 pace competitive.",
